# BFF proxies (must come BEFORE SPA catch-all)
#
# ORDERING RULE — the one thing to know before adding a proxy path here.
# Netlify processes THIS FILE BEFORE netlify.toml's [[redirects]]. This file
# ends in the SPA catch-all `/*  /index.html  200`, so a proxy path declared
# ONLY in netlify.toml [[redirects]] is dead — the catch-all answers it with
# the SPA index (HTTP 200 text/html) and the request never leaves Netlify.
# A proxy path is reachable only if it is declared ABOVE the catch-all in this
# file, or bound to an [[edge_functions]] path (edge functions run first).
# tests/ci-guards/netlify-bff-route-precedence.spec.ts fails on any /bff/*
# path that satisfies neither.
#
# Seams NOT declared in this file, because each needs a request header injected
# and this file cannot inject headers — they are edge functions instead:
#   /bff/isl/*         → netlify/edge-functions/isl-proxy.ts        (ISL_API_KEY)
#   /bff/orchestrate/* → netlify/edge-functions/orchestrator-proxy.ts (ASSIST_API_KEY)
#   /bff/cee/*         → netlify/edge-functions/cee-proxy.ts        (ASSIST_API_KEY)
#   /bff/engine/*      → netlify/edge-functions/plot-proxy.ts       (PLOT_AUTH_TOKEN)
#   /engine/*          → netlify/edge-functions/plot-proxy.ts       (PLOT_AUTH_TOKEN)
#
# The last two were plain rewrites here until the bundle-credential fix. A rewrite
# cannot inject a header, so PLoT's auth flip was survived by shipping the bearer to
# the BROWSER (VITE_PLOT_BEARER), which Vite inlined as a bare literal into a public
# asset — a live server-to-server credential any visitor could fetch. The rewrite was
# not wrong about routing; it was simply incapable of being a credential boundary,
# and that incapacity was paid for in the client bundle.
#
# Until ROADMAP 2.317 the third line above read "/bff/cee/* handled by
# netlify.toml redirect with header injection". That was false, and the line
# directly below it was what made it false: the catch-all outranked the
# netlify.toml rule, so /bff/cee/* served SPA HTML on every deploy. A comment
# asserting a guarantee that the adjacent config defeats is the defect class
# here — hence the guard, which derives reachability instead of trusting prose.
# Static routes
/poc           /poc.html                                               200
/fixtures/*    /fixtures/:splat                                        200

# Immutable, content-hashed build output — MUST 404 WHEN ABSENT.
#
# Vite emits `/assets/<name>-<hash>.js|.css` and every deploy retires the
# previous deploy's hashes. Without this rule the catch-all below answers a
# retired chunk with the SPA index — HTTP 200, content-type text/html — so a
# browser that loaded the app BEFORE a deploy fails its next lazy import with
# "Expected a JavaScript module script but the server responded with a MIME
# type of text/html", and the feature is dead in that tab until a reload.
# Nothing 404s, so no status-code monitor can see it. Measured on staging at
# 07a807a3: three once-deployed chunk paths and a deliberately fabricated one
# all returned 200 text/html; hardNotFound was 0 across an 81-path crawl.
#
# ⚠ UNFORCED ON PURPOSE. Netlify shadowing means an unforced rule does not
# apply when a real file exists at the path, so assets that WERE built are
# still served normally and only absent ones reach this rule — Netlify
# documents exactly this pattern ("the 404 pages will only be returned for
# nonexistent assets under the above paths"). Adding `!` would force it,
# defeat shadowing, and 404 EVERY asset, i.e. take the site down.
#
# It lives HERE and not in netlify.toml for the ordering reason at the top of
# this file: a netlify.toml [[redirects]] rule for this path would be dead on
# arrival, outranked by the catch-all — the same duplicate-declaration shape
# that made /bff/cee/* unreachable for months.
#
# tests/ci-guards/netlify-asset-404-precedence.spec.ts derives the prefix from
# vite.config.ts `assetsDir` and fails if this rule is missing, mis-statused,
# forced, or sitting below the catch-all.
/assets/*      /404.html                                               404

# SPA catch-all (MUST BE LAST - catches all unmatched routes)
/*             /index.html                                             200
